// Recall 14 + Reliability Drill: the 14 recall Qs Sihan typed on WhatsApp on 5 Aug 2026 (exam not named, so the
// stems are his and the options are rebuilt to test the same point) + an 18-Q drill on Spearman-Brown, Rulon, KR-20/21,
// speed tests, variance, SEM and attenuation. Numeric keys checked in Python. Own file so a data-all.js rebuild can't drop it.
// The deep overlay (concept + why each wrong option is wrong) is index-matched below. Also read by /recall-14/ (the why page).
window.PREVIEW_MOCKS = window.PREVIEW_MOCKS || {};
window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["recall-14"] = {"id": "recall-14", "title": "Recall 14 + Reliability Drill", "fullTitle": "Recall 14 + Reliability Drill (your 5 Aug recall set + 18 formula Qs)", "sub": "32 questions · your 14 recall Qs + 18 reliability and variance drill Qs · untimed", "sectionLabels": {"recall": "Your 14 recall questions", "drill": "Reliability + variance drill"}, "questions": [{"q": "The Müller-Lyer illusion is best explained by:", "options": {"a": "Misapplied size constancy (inappropriate constancy scaling)", "b": "Binocular disparity between the two eyes' images", "c": "Lateral inhibition between neighbouring retinal cells", "d": "Figure-ground reversal"}, "correct": "a", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is misapplied size constancy, Richard Gregory's inappropriate constancy scaling theory (1963, 1968). Franz Carl Müller-Lyer published the illusion in 1889: two lines of equal length, one ending in outward-flaring fins and one ending in arrowheads. The finned line looks longer. Gregory's account is that the visual system reads the flat figures as 3-D corners. The arrowhead line looks like the outside corner of a building jutting toward you, so it is read as nearer. The finned line looks like the inside corner of a room, receding away from you, so it is read as farther. Size constancy then scales up whatever seems farther: if two lines cast the same retinal image and one is farther away, it must be bigger, so the finned line looks longer. A mechanism built for the 3-D world is applied to a flat drawing where it does not belong, hence misapplied. Supporting evidence: Marshall Segall, Donald Campbell and Melville Herskovits (1966) found that people raised in carpentered environments full of right angles are more susceptible than groups such as the Zulu, who live in round huts and see fewer straight corners, the carpentered-world hypothesis. The standard criticism is that the illusion survives when the fins are swapped for circles or dots, which carry no depth cue, so Gregory is not the whole story. For the exam, the keyed answer is misapplied size constancy, also worded as depth cues or constancy scaling.", "topic": "Müller-Lyer illusion: misapplied size constancy (Gregory)"}, {"q": "A lesion of the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) produces:", "options": {"a": "Loss of circadian rhythms", "b": "Aphagia and adipsia", "c": "Diabetes insipidus", "d": "Hyperphagia and obesity"}, "correct": "d", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is hyperphagia and obesity, the VMH syndrome first shown in rats by Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson (1940). The classic dual-centre model (Eliot Stellar, 1954) called the ventromedial hypothalamus the satiety centre and the lateral hypothalamus (LH) the feeding centre. Destroy the VMH and the animal overeats and becomes obese. Destroy the LH (Bal Krishan Anand and John Brobeck, 1951) and it stops eating and drinking. The VMH rat goes through two phases: a dynamic phase of heavy overeating and rapid weight gain, then a static phase where weight levels off at a new, high point and intake drops back toward normal. It also becomes a finicky eater, eating more of tasty food and less of bitter food than a normal rat. The modern view in John Pinel's Biopsychology is that the dual-centre idea is too simple. Much of the effect comes from damage to the nearby paraventricular nucleus and the fibre tracts passing the VMH, and VMH lesions raise blood insulin. High insulin drives lipogenesis (storing fat) and blocks lipolysis (releasing it), so the animal has to keep eating to have enough usable energy in the blood. Lock it as: VMH lesion makes the animal fat, LH lesion makes it thin.", "topic": "VMH lesion: hyperphagia and obesity (LH lesion = aphagia)"}, {"q": "Bilateral removal of the amygdala in monkeys produces:", "options": {"a": "Hyperphagia and obesity", "b": "Intense fear, attack posture and piloerection", "c": "Severe anterograde amnesia for facts and events, as in H.M.", "d": "Tameness and loss of fear, with hyperorality and hypersexuality"}, "correct": "d", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is tameness and loss of fear, the Klüver-Bucy syndrome. Heinrich Klüver and Paul Bucy (1937, 1939) removed both temporal lobes in rhesus monkeys, and later work tied the emotional part of the syndrome to the amygdala. The full picture: tameness and loss of fear (the monkeys approached snakes and people they used to flee), psychic blindness or visual agnosia (they could see objects but not recognise what they meant), hyperorality (putting everything in the mouth), hypersexuality, dietary changes (eating meat and inedible things), and hypermetamorphosis (compulsively attending to and touching everything in view). The trap your stem sets: fear, attack posture and piloerection (hair standing on end) are what STIMULATION of the amygdala produces, the defensive rage response. A lesion takes them away. So if the options are fear, attack posture, piloerection and a fourth like tameness or loss of all of these, the stem word lesion points to the loss. In humans, bilateral amygdala damage in patient S.M. (Urbach-Wiethe disease, Ralph Adolphs and colleagues, 1994) wiped out recognising fear in faces and the feeling of fear itself. Joseph LeDoux's fear-conditioning circuit runs through the amygdala, which is why losing it removes learned fear.", "topic": "Bilateral amygdala lesion: Klüver-Bucy (tameness, loss of fear)"}, {"q": "Scores of 8 students: 2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9. Using σ² = Σ(X − M)² / N, the variance is:", "options": {"a": "4", "b": "2", "c": "32", "d": "5"}, "correct": "a", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is 4. Step 1, the mean: M = ΣX / N = 40 / 8 = 5. Step 2, deviations X − M: −3, −1, −1, −1, 0, 0, 2, 4. Step 3, square them: 9, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 4, 16. Their sum, the sum of squares (SS), is 32. Step 4, divide by N: 32 / 8 = 4. The standard deviation is its square root, σ = 2. Variance is the average squared distance from the mean, so it is in squared units; the SD brings it back to score units. Two versions to know. Population variance σ² = SS / N is what Henry Garrett and most Indian psychometrics texts use for describing a set of scores. The unbiased sample estimate s² = SS / (N − 1) would give 32 / 7 = 4.57 here, and you use it when the paper says sample or estimate the population. Shortcut for raw data: σ² = ΣX² / N − M². Here ΣX² = 232, 232 / 8 = 29, and 29 − 25 = 4, the same answer. Quick rules: variance is never negative, it is zero only when every score is equal, adding a constant to every score leaves it unchanged, and multiplying every score by c multiplies it by c².", "topic": "Calculating variance: σ² = Σ(X − M)² / N"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a non-linear transformation of raw scores?", "options": {"a": "z-score", "b": "50 + 10z", "c": "Stanine", "d": "Deviation score (X − M)"}, "correct": "c", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is stanine. A linear transformation changes every score by the same multiply-and-add rule (new = a + b × old). It keeps the shape of the distribution, and the distances between scores stay in proportion, so a skewed raw distribution stays skewed. z = (X − M) / SD, deviation scores and the linear T-score 50 + 10z are all linear. A non-linear transformation, also called an area or normalising transformation, goes through percentile ranks: each raw score becomes the proportion of people below it, and that proportion is mapped onto a normal curve. This forces the distribution into a normal shape, so the spacing between scores changes. The non-linear family: percentile ranks, normalised standard scores, McCall's T-scale (William McCall, 1922, built as a normalised scale), stanines (standard nine, mean 5, SD about 2, first used by the US Army Air Forces in World War II), stens (standard ten, mean 5.5, SD 2) and normal-curve equivalents. The T trap: 50 + 10z is linear, while a normalised or McCall's T is non-linear, which is why some keys list T-scores as non-linear. Percentile ranks stretch and squeeze the most: near the middle of a normal curve a small raw-score change moves the percentile a lot, while in the tails a big raw change barely moves it.", "topic": "Non-linear transformations: percentile ranks, stanines, normalised T"}, {"q": "The Spearman-Brown prophecy formula is used to:", "options": {"a": "Estimate agreement between raters on categorical judgements", "b": "Correct a validity coefficient for unreliability in the test and the criterion", "c": "Estimate the reliability of a speed test from a single sitting", "d": "Estimate full-test reliability from a split-half correlation, or predict reliability when test length changes"}, "correct": "d", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is d. Charles Spearman and William Brown published it independently in 1910, in the same issue of the British Journal of Psychology. Formula: r_new = k × r / (1 + (k − 1) × r), where r is the current reliability and k is how many times longer or shorter the new test is. Its commonest use is split-half reliability. You split one test into two halves (usually odd versus even items) and correlate them. That correlation is the reliability of a HALF-length test, and a shorter test is less reliable, so you step it up to full length with k = 2: r_full = 2r / (1 + r). The rules it rests on: the two halves, or the items being added, must be parallel to the existing ones (same content, same difficulty, equal means and equal variances), they must measure the same thing, and errors must be random and uncorrelated between halves. How it is applied: lengthening with parallel items always raises reliability, with diminishing returns; shortening (k less than 1) lowers it; and you can solve for k to find how long a test must be to reach a target, k = r_target (1 − r) / (r (1 − r_target)). Where it fails: when the halves have unequal variances it overestimates, so use Rulon's or Flanagan's formula instead, and on a speed test the halves correlate near 1 for the wrong reason, so the stepped-up split-half is spuriously high. The drill section of this mock has the numbers.", "topic": "Spearman-Brown prophecy formula: use, rules and assumptions"}, {"q": "Kuder-Richardson Formula 20 (KR-20) is the appropriate reliability estimate when:", "options": {"a": "The same test is given twice, two weeks apart", "b": "Items are scored right/wrong (dichotomous) and vary in difficulty", "c": "The test is a pure speed test", "d": "Items are on a 5-point Likert scale"}, "correct": "b", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is b. G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937) derived a set of internal-consistency formulas from a single administration, and the 20th and 21st in their paper became the famous ones. KR-20 = (k / (k − 1)) × (1 − Σpq / σ²), where k is the number of items, p is the proportion passing each item, q = 1 − p, pq is that item's variance, and σ² is the variance of total scores. The logic: if the items all measure the same thing, they covary, so the total-score variance is much bigger than the sum of the separate item variances. Σpq / σ² is then small and KR-20 is high. It equals the average of all possible split-half coefficients, so no arbitrary split is needed. Conditions: items scored 0 or 1, one trait being measured (homogeneous content), and a power test, not a speed test. KR-21 is the shortcut that replaces Σpq with M(k − M) / k, which assumes every item has the same difficulty; when difficulties differ, KR-21 comes out lower than KR-20. Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) is the general version for any item format, and with 0/1 items alpha equals KR-20 exactly. A high KR-20 means the items hang together. It does not prove a single factor and it says nothing about stability over time.", "topic": "KR-20: internal consistency for dichotomous items"}, {"q": "Rulon's formula estimates split-half reliability from:", "options": {"a": "The variance of the differences between the two half-scores, relative to the total-score variance", "b": "The correlation between two administrations of the same test", "c": "The correlation between the halves, stepped up with Spearman-Brown", "d": "The proportion of examinees passing each item"}, "correct": "a", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is a. Phillip Rulon (1939): r = 1 − σ²_d / σ²_x, where d is each person's difference score (odd-half score minus even-half score) and σ²_x is the variance of total scores. The logic: if both halves measure the same true score, any difference between them is error. So the variance of the differences estimates error variance, and 1 minus the error share of total variance is reliability, straight from the classical definition r = 1 − σ²_e / σ²_x. Why it matters: it gives the full-test reliability directly, so you do NOT step it up with Spearman-Brown afterwards, and it does not need the two halves to have equal variances, which Spearman-Brown does. Flanagan's formula (John Flanagan, 1937) is the equivalent version written with the half variances, r = 2 (1 − (σ²_a + σ²_b) / σ²_x), and Louis Guttman's split-half coefficient (1945) is the same quantity. When the halves happen to have equal variances, Rulon, Flanagan and Spearman-Brown all give the same answer. Common exam angles: which split-half formula uses the variance of difference scores (Rulon), and which split-half formulas need no correlation coefficient (Rulon and Flanagan).", "topic": "Rulon's split-half formula: 1 − σ²d / σ²x"}, {"q": "For a pure speed test, the LEAST appropriate way to estimate reliability is:", "options": {"a": "Alternate (parallel) forms", "b": "Test-retest", "c": "Two separately timed half-tests", "d": "Odd-even split-half from a single timed sitting"}, "correct": "d", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is odd-even split-half from one sitting. A pure speed test has easy items and a tight time limit, so the score is simply how far you got. Almost everyone gets every item they reach right and every item they do not reach wrong. Split that into odd and even items and each person's two half-scores are nearly identical by construction: if you reached item 60, you have about 30 odd and 30 even right. The correlation between halves comes out near 1.00, not because the test is consistent but because of how speed scores are built. The same inflation hits KR-20, KR-21 and Cronbach's alpha, the other single-sitting internal-consistency methods; this is Anne Anastasi's standard warning in Psychological Testing. What to use instead: test-retest (coefficient of stability), alternate forms (coefficient of equivalence), or, if you must split, give the two halves separately with their own time limits, then correlate them and step up with Spearman-Brown. Exam variants: spuriously high reliability on a speed test comes from split-half or internal consistency; the most suitable methods for a speed test are test-retest and alternate forms.", "topic": "Speed tests: split-half and internal consistency are spuriously high"}, {"q": "Circumstantiality is a disorder of:", "options": {"a": "Possession of thought", "b": "Content of thought", "c": "Form of thought (thought process)", "d": "Perception"}, "correct": "c", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is form of thought. In circumstantiality the patient takes a long, roundabout route through unnecessary detail and side-tracks, but eventually reaches the point and answers the question. Contrast tangentiality, where the patient drifts off and never reaches the goal. It is seen in epilepsy (classically temporal lobe), obsessional personalities, intellectual disability, early dementia and in anxious or pedantic people, and a mild form can be normal. The four classic headings for disorders of thought come from Frank Fish's Clinical Psychopathology and are used in Indian texts: stream (speed and flow, such as flight of ideas, pressure of thought, poverty of thought, thought block), form (how thoughts link, such as loosening of association, derailment, tangentiality, circumstantiality, neologism, perseveration, word salad), content (what is thought, such as delusions, overvalued ideas, preoccupations) and possession (ownership of thought, such as thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting; Fish also puts obsessions here). Most keys, and Kaplan and Sadock, put circumstantiality under form, as a formal thought disorder. Fish himself listed it under disorders of the tempo of thought, which is stream. So: if form is an option, pick form; if only stream fits, pick stream. It is never content or possession.", "topic": "Circumstantiality: disorder of form of thought (Fish: stream)"}, {"q": "The Rights of Persons with Disabilities (RPwD) Act was passed in ____ and recognises ____ disabilities.", "options": {"a": "1995; 7", "b": "2016; 19", "c": "2016; 21", "d": "2017; 21"}, "correct": "c", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is 2016 and 21. The RPwD Act, 2016 (Act No. 49 of 2016) received Presidential assent on 27 December 2016 and came into force on 19 April 2017. It replaced the Persons with Disabilities (Equal Opportunities, Protection of Rights and Full Participation) Act, 1995, which recognised only 7 disabilities: blindness, low vision, leprosy-cured, hearing impairment, locomotor disability, mental retardation and mental illness. It was passed to bring Indian law in line with the UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (UNCRPD, 2006), which India ratified in 2007. The 21 are: blindness, low vision, leprosy-cured persons, hearing impairment (deaf and hard of hearing), locomotor disability, dwarfism, intellectual disability, mental illness, autism spectrum disorder, cerebral palsy, muscular dystrophy, chronic neurological conditions, specific learning disabilities, multiple sclerosis, speech and language disability, thalassemia, haemophilia, sickle cell disease, multiple disabilities including deaf-blindness, acid attack victims, and Parkinson's disease. Benchmark disability means at least 40% of a specified disability, which unlocks reservation: 4% of government posts (Section 34) and 5% of seats in government and aided higher education (Section 32). Points that matter for psychology: the Act uses intellectual disability in place of mental retardation, it added autism and specific learning disabilities, and the central government can notify further categories.", "topic": "RPwD Act 2016: 21 disabilities (PwD Act 1995 had 7)"}, {"q": "The right to make an advance directive about mental health treatment is given by the:", "options": {"a": "Mental Healthcare Act, 2017", "b": "Mental Health Act, 1987", "c": "Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, 2016", "d": "Rehabilitation Council of India Act, 1992"}, "correct": "a", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is the Mental Healthcare Act (MHCA), 2017, Act No. 10 of 2017, assented on 7 April 2017 and in force from 29 May 2018. Chapter III, Sections 5 to 13, lets every adult write an advance directive saying how they want, or do not want, to be cared for and treated for a mental illness, and Section 14 lets them appoint a nominated representative. The directive is made in writing, can be changed or revoked at any time, is registered with the Mental Health Review Board, and does not apply to emergency treatment. Other high-yield MHCA sections: Section 18, the right to access mental healthcare; Section 89, supported admission for up to 30 days; Section 94, emergency treatment for up to 72 hours; Section 95, banned procedures (ECT without anaesthesia and muscle relaxant, ECT for minors unless the guardian consents and the Board permits, sterilisation as treatment, chaining); and Section 115, which presumes severe stress in anyone who attempts suicide, so the attempt is not punished. The Act replaced the Mental Health Act, 1987 and set up the Central and State Mental Health Authorities and the Review Boards. Both the MHCA and the RPwD Act grew out of the UNCRPD, which is why papers pair them.", "topic": "Advance directives: Mental Healthcare Act 2017 (Sections 5 to 13)"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT listed as a specified disability under the RPwD Act, 2016?", "options": {"a": "Specific learning disabilities", "b": "Intellectual disability", "c": "Attention deficit hyperactivity disorder (ADHD)", "d": "Autism spectrum disorder"}, "correct": "c", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is ADHD. The Act's schedule groups its 21 specified disabilities under five heads. Physical disability covers locomotor disability (including leprosy-cured persons, cerebral palsy, dwarfism, muscular dystrophy and acid attack victims), visual impairment (blindness, low vision), hearing impairment (deaf, hard of hearing) and speech and language disability. Intellectual disability has specific learning disabilities and autism spectrum disorder listed under it. Mental behaviour covers mental illness. Disability caused by chronic neurological conditions (multiple sclerosis, Parkinson's disease) and blood disorders (haemophilia, thalassemia, sickle cell disease) comes next, and the last head is multiple disabilities, including deaf-blindness. ADHD is not named anywhere in the schedule. The trick in this question is that all four options are neurodevelopmental disorders in DSM-5, and three of them made it into the Act. The specific learning disabilities named in the Act are dyslexia, dysgraphia, dyscalculia, dyspraxia, developmental aphasia and perceptual disabilities.", "topic": "RPwD Act: ADHD is not a specified disability (ASD, ID, SLD are)"}, {"q": "In James Gross's process model of emotion regulation, cognitive reappraisal belongs to which family of strategies?", "options": {"a": "Response modulation", "b": "Cognitive change", "c": "Situation selection", "d": "Attentional deployment"}, "correct": "b", "section": "recall", "explanation": "The answer is cognitive change. Your stem was cut off at Emotional Regulatio, so this is the emotion-regulation question papers ask most. James Gross (1998) defines emotion regulation as the processes by which we influence which emotions we have, when we have them, and how we experience and express them. His process model has five families of strategies, ordered by when they act along the emotion's timeline. Situation selection: choosing to enter or avoid a situation, like skipping a party you dread. Situation modification: changing the situation itself, like asking a friend to come along. Attentional deployment: shifting attention, as in distraction or rumination. Cognitive change: changing how you appraise the situation, and cognitive reappraisal is the main example (the interviewer is tired, not hostile). Response modulation: acting on the emotion once it is under way, as in expressive suppression, exercise or drugs. The first four are antecedent-focused, acting before the emotion fully forms; the fifth is response-focused. Gross and Oliver John (2003) built the 10-item Emotion Regulation Questionnaire (ERQ) to measure reappraisal and suppression. Reappraisal lowers the felt negative emotion and goes with better wellbeing. Suppression hides the expression but not the feeling, raises physiological arousal and hurts memory and relationships. Clinical link: Marsha Linehan's dialectical behaviour therapy has an emotion regulation skills module, and emotion dysregulation is the core problem in her biosocial model of borderline personality disorder.", "topic": "Gross's process model of emotion regulation: reappraisal vs suppression"}, {"q": "The correlation between the odd and even halves of a test is .60. By the Spearman-Brown formula, the reliability of the full test is:", "options": {"a": ".80", "b": ".60", "c": ".75", "d": ".36"}, "correct": "c", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is .75. The split-half correlation of .60 is the reliability of a half-length test. To get the full test you double the length, so k = 2: r_full = 2r / (1 + r) = 2 × .60 / (1 + .60) = 1.20 / 1.60 = .75. The general formula is r_new = k r / (1 + (k − 1) r). Sanity check: the stepped-up value is always higher than the half-test r, because lengthening helps, but it never reaches 1.", "topic": "Spearman-Brown: split-half step-up 2r / (1 + r)"}, {"q": "A test has a reliability of .50. If it is made three times as long with parallel items, its predicted reliability is:", "options": {"a": ".83", "b": ".60", "c": "1.00", "d": ".75"}, "correct": "d", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is .75. Here k = 3, so r_new = k r / (1 + (k − 1) r) = 3 × .50 / (1 + 2 × .50) = 1.50 / 2.00 = .75. Notice the diminishing returns: doubling (k = 2) gives 1.00 / 1.50 = .67, tripling gives .75, and five times the length gives 2.50 / 3.00 = .83. Each extra block of items adds less, and reliability only approaches 1 as the length heads to infinity.", "topic": "Spearman-Brown: lengthening a test (k = 3)"}, {"q": "A test has a reliability of .60. How many times longer must it be made, with parallel items, to reach a reliability of .90?", "options": {"a": "3 times", "b": "6 times", "c": "1.5 times", "d": "9 times"}, "correct": "b", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is 6 times. Rearranged Spearman-Brown: k = r_target (1 − r_old) / (r_old (1 − r_target)) = .90 × .40 / (.60 × .10) = .36 / .06 = 6. Check by plugging it back in: 6 × .60 / (1 + 5 × .60) = 3.60 / 4.00 = .90. So a 20-item test would need 120 parallel items. This is why a short screening tool with reliability around .60 cannot be pushed to the .90 level needed for individual clinical decisions just by adding a few items.", "topic": "Spearman-Brown: how much longer to reach a target reliability"}, {"q": "A test with a reliability of .80 is cut to half its length. The predicted reliability of the short form is:", "options": {"a": ".40", "b": ".67", "c": ".89", "d": ".80"}, "correct": "b", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is .67. Spearman-Brown works for shortening too, with k less than 1. With k = 0.5: r_new = 0.5 × .80 / (1 + (0.5 − 1) × .80) = .40 / (1 − .40) = .40 / .60 = .67. It is the reverse of the split-half step-up, and you can check it: stepping .67 back up with k = 2 gives 1.33 / 1.67 = .80. Use it whenever someone wants a short form of a test.", "topic": "Spearman-Brown: shortening a test (k = 0.5)"}, {"q": "The Spearman-Brown correction of a split-half coefficient is accurate only when the two halves:", "options": {"a": "Are parallel: equal means and equal variances, measuring the same content", "b": "Differ in difficulty, so the range of scores is wider", "c": "Are scored by two different examiners", "d": "Are given on two different days"}, "correct": "a", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is a. Spearman-Brown assumes the halves are parallel tests: the same true score and equal error variance, which means equal means and equal observed variances. Only then is the correlation between halves a fair estimate of each half's reliability. If the halves have unequal variances, for example one half holding most of the hard items, Spearman-Brown overestimates reliability. Then you use Rulon's formula (1939), 1 − σ²_d / σ²_x, or Flanagan's (1937), 2 (1 − (σ²_a + σ²_b) / σ²_x), which only assume the halves measure the same true score, not that their variances match. How to make halves parallel: an odd-even split spreads difficulty evenly when items run from easy to hard, or match items on content and difficulty. A first-half versus second-half split is the worst, because fatigue, practice and time pressure all fall on the second half.", "topic": "Spearman-Brown assumption: parallel halves (else Rulon/Flanagan)"}, {"q": "On a test split into odd and even halves, the variance of the difference scores (odd minus even) is 4 and the variance of total scores is 20. By Rulon's formula, the reliability is:", "options": {"a": ".20", "b": ".80", "c": ".96", "d": ".89"}, "correct": "b", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is .80. Rulon: r = 1 − σ²_d / σ²_x = 1 − 4 / 20 = 1 − .20 = .80. The difference between the halves is treated as pure error, so 4 / 20 = .20 is the error share of the total variance, and the rest is reliable variance. Rulon's result is already the full-test reliability, so there is no Spearman-Brown step afterwards.", "topic": "Rulon's formula with variances: 1 − σ²d / σ²x"}, {"q": "In a split-half analysis, the standard deviation of the difference scores is 3 and the standard deviation of total scores is 6. Rulon's reliability coefficient is:", "options": {"a": ".75", "b": ".86", "c": ".50", "d": ".25"}, "correct": "a", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is .75. Rulon's formula needs VARIANCES, so square the SDs first: σ²_d = 3² = 9 and σ²_x = 6² = 36. Then r = 1 − 9 / 36 = 1 − .25 = .75. Papers give SDs on purpose to catch anyone who plugs them straight in.", "topic": "Rulon's formula given SDs: square them first"}, {"q": "A 10-item right/wrong test has Σpq = 1.8 and a total-score variance of 6. Its KR-20 reliability is about:", "options": {"a": ".30", "b": ".78", "c": ".70", "d": ".63"}, "correct": "b", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is .78. KR-20 = (k / (k − 1)) × (1 − Σpq / σ²). Step 1: Σpq / σ² = 1.8 / 6 = .30. Step 2: 1 − .30 = .70. Step 3: the correction factor k / (k − 1) = 10 / 9 = 1.111. Step 4: 1.111 × .70 = .78. The k / (k − 1) factor matters most on short tests: with 10 items it adds about 11%, with 100 items only about 1%.", "topic": "KR-20 calculation: (k / (k − 1)) × (1 − Σpq / σ²)"}, {"q": "On a right/wrong item, 30% of examinees answer correctly. The item variance (pq) that goes into KR-20 is:", "options": {"a": ".25", "b": ".09", "c": ".21", "d": ".30"}, "correct": "c", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is .21. For a dichotomous item, p is the proportion correct (.30), q = 1 − p = .70, and the item variance is p × q = .30 × .70 = .21. Item variance peaks at p = .50 (.50 × .50 = .25) and falls to 0 when everyone passes or everyone fails. That is why items of middling difficulty, p around .50, carry the most information and discriminate best, and why item analysis prefers them. In KR-20 you add pq across all the items to get Σpq.", "topic": "Item variance for a right/wrong item: pq (maximum .25 at p = .50)"}, {"q": "A 20-item test has a mean of 12 and a variance of 16. Its KR-21 reliability is about:", "options": {"a": ".30", "b": ".70", "c": ".85", "d": ".74"}, "correct": "d", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is .74. KR-21 = (k / (k − 1)) × (1 − M (k − M) / (k σ²)), and it needs only the number of items, the mean and the variance, with no item-by-item data. Step 1: M (k − M) = 12 × 8 = 96. Step 2: k σ² = 20 × 16 = 320. Step 3: 96 / 320 = .30, so 1 − .30 = .70. Step 4: multiply by 20 / 19 = 1.053, which gives .74. KR-21 assumes every item has the same difficulty. Real items never do, so KR-21 comes out lower than KR-20 and works as a quick, conservative estimate.", "topic": "KR-21 calculation from mean and variance"}, {"q": "When the items of a test differ in difficulty, KR-21 compared with KR-20 gives:", "options": {"a": "Exactly the same estimate", "b": "An estimate of test-retest stability instead", "c": "A lower estimate", "d": "A higher estimate"}, "correct": "c", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is a lower estimate. KR-21 swaps the real Σpq for k × p̄q̄, the value Σpq would take if every item had the average difficulty. Because pq is largest at p = .50 and curves down on both sides, spreading item difficulties around the average always makes the real Σpq SMALLER than k × p̄q̄. KR-20 subtracts the smaller real value, so it comes out higher; KR-21 subtracts the larger assumed value, so it comes out lower. The two are equal only when every item has exactly the same difficulty. So KR-21 is a lower-bound shortcut: if KR-21 already looks good, KR-20 will be at least as good.", "topic": "KR-21 vs KR-20: KR-21 is lower when item difficulties vary"}, {"q": "Cronbach's coefficient alpha is identical to KR-20 when:", "options": {"a": "The test is a speed test", "b": "All items are scored dichotomously (0 or 1)", "c": "The test is given twice", "d": "The two halves have equal variances"}, "correct": "b", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is b. Lee Cronbach (1951) generalised KR-20 to any item format: α = (k / (k − 1)) × (1 − Σσ²_i / σ²_x), where σ²_i is each item's variance. For a 0/1 item the item variance is exactly pq, so Σσ²_i = Σpq and alpha becomes KR-20 term for term. Cronbach also showed that alpha is the mean of all possible split-half coefficients (computed the Rulon-Flanagan way), and it is a lower bound to true reliability when items are not equally good measures of the trait. Use alpha for Likert and rating items and KR-20 for right/wrong items. Jum Nunnally's rule of thumb: .70 is acceptable for early research, .80 for most research, and .90 or above for decisions about individuals.", "topic": "Cronbach's alpha equals KR-20 for dichotomous items"}, {"q": "A clerical speed test has to be split in half to estimate its reliability. The acceptable procedure is to:", "options": {"a": "Time the two halves separately, correlate them and step up with Spearman-Brown", "b": "Score odd and even items from one timed sitting and step up with Spearman-Brown", "c": "Compute Cronbach's alpha from the single sitting", "d": "Compute KR-20 from the single sitting"}, "correct": "a", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is a. On a speed test, any split inside one timed sitting gives two nearly identical half-scores, because everyone gets right whatever they reach and the score only tracks how far they got. The fix, Anne Anastasi's standard recommendation, is to give each half as its own timed test with half the time limit. Then how far you get in half A does not decide your score on half B, so the correlation reflects real consistency. Correct that half-test r to full length with Spearman-Brown (k = 2). The other good options for speed tests avoid splitting altogether: test-retest and alternate forms.", "topic": "Speed test split-half: time the halves separately"}, {"q": "Scores of 3, 5 and 7 are a sample. The unbiased estimate of the population variance (dividing by N − 1) is:", "options": {"a": "2.67", "b": "4", "c": "2", "d": "8"}, "correct": "b", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is 4. Mean = 15 / 3 = 5. Deviations: −2, 0, 2. Squares: 4, 0, 4, so SS = 8. The unbiased sample variance is s² = SS / (N − 1) = 8 / 2 = 4. Why N − 1: deviations from a sample's own mean are always a little smaller than deviations from the true population mean, because the sample mean sits in the middle of its own data, so dividing by N underestimates the population variance. Dividing by N − 1 (Bessel's correction) removes that bias. N − 1 is also the degrees of freedom: once you know the mean and two of the three deviations, the third is fixed.", "topic": "Sample variance: divide by N − 1 (Bessel's correction)"}, {"q": "A set of scores has a variance of 4. Every score is multiplied by 3 and then 10 is added. The new variance is:", "options": {"a": "36", "b": "4", "c": "12", "d": "46"}, "correct": "a", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is 36. Adding a constant shifts every score and the mean by the same amount, so every deviation from the mean stays the same and the variance does not change. Multiplying by a constant c multiplies every deviation by c, so the squared deviations, and the variance, are multiplied by c². Here 4 × 3² = 4 × 9 = 36, and the +10 changes nothing. The SD goes from 2 to 6, multiplied by 3. The mean takes both changes: new mean = 3 × old mean + 10. This is the rule behind linear transformations (item 5 of your recall set): z, linear T and deviation-score scales change the mean and SD this way without changing the shape of the distribution.", "topic": "Variance under a linear transformation: × c gives × c², + c gives no change"}, {"q": "A test has an SD of 15 and a reliability of .91. The standard error of measurement (SEM) is:", "options": {"a": "0.3", "b": "1.35", "c": "13.65", "d": "4.5"}, "correct": "d", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is 4.5. SEM = SD × √(1 − r) = 15 × √(1 − .91) = 15 × √.09 = 15 × .30 = 4.5. The SEM is the SD of the errors you would get if one person took the test over and over, so it gives a band around the observed score where the true score probably lies: about 68% of the time within ±1 SEM (±4.5) and 95% within ±1.96 SEM (about ±8.8). On this test an observed IQ of 110 means the true IQ is probably between about 101 and 119. Higher reliability means a smaller SEM: at r = 1 the SEM is 0, and at r = 0 it equals the SD.", "topic": "Standard error of measurement: SD × √(1 − r)"}, {"q": "A predictor and a criterion correlate .40. Their reliabilities are .64 and .81. Corrected for attenuation, the correlation between their true scores is about:", "options": {"a": ".77", "b": ".29", "c": ".56", "d": ".40"}, "correct": "c", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is .56. Charles Spearman's correction for attenuation (1904): r_true = r_xy / √(r_xx × r_yy) = .40 / √(.64 × .81) = .40 / (.80 × .90) = .40 / .72 = .56. Measurement error in either test drags the observed correlation down, attenuating it, and the formula estimates what the correlation would be with perfectly reliable measures. Two follow-ups papers like: a test's validity coefficient can never exceed the square root of its reliability (here √.64 = .80 is the ceiling for the predictor), and in validity studies you often correct only for the criterion's unreliability (.40 / √.81 = .40 / .90 = .44), so the test is not credited with a perfection it does not have.", "topic": "Correction for attenuation: r_xy / √(r_xx × r_yy)"}, {"q": "On the stanine scale, what percentage of a normal distribution falls in stanine 5?", "options": {"a": "17%", "b": "10%", "c": "25%", "d": "20%"}, "correct": "d", "section": "drill", "explanation": "The answer is 20%. The stanine (standard nine) scale cuts the normal curve into nine bands, each half an SD wide except the two open-ended ends, with a mean of 5 and an SD of about 2. The percentages from stanine 1 to 9 are 4, 7, 12, 17, 20, 17, 12, 7, 4, adding to 100. Stanine 5 is the middle band, from −0.25 to +0.25 SD, and holds 20%. Stanines are assigned from percentile ranks, so they are a normalising, non-linear transformation (item 5 of your recall set). They trade precision for robustness: one digit, no false precision. The scale was developed for the US Army Air Forces in World War II. Its cousin, the sten (standard ten, mean 5.5, SD 2), is used by Raymond Cattell's 16PF.", "topic": "Stanine bands: 4, 7, 12, 17, 20, 17, 12, 7, 4 percent"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP || {}, {"recall-14": [{"q": "The Müller-Lyer illusion is best explained by:", "concept": "The answer is misapplied size constancy, Richard Gregory's inappropriate constancy scaling theory (1963, 1968). Franz Carl Müller-Lyer published the illusion in 1889: two lines of equal length, one ending in outward-flaring fins and one ending in arrowheads. The finned line looks longer. Gregory's account is that the visual system reads the flat figures as 3-D corners. The arrowhead line looks like the outside corner of a building jutting toward you, so it is read as nearer. The finned line looks like the inside corner of a room, receding away from you, so it is read as farther. Size constancy then scales up whatever seems farther: if two lines cast the same retinal image and one is farther away, it must be bigger, so the finned line looks longer. A mechanism built for the 3-D world is applied to a flat drawing where it does not belong, hence misapplied. Supporting evidence: Marshall Segall, Donald Campbell and Melville Herskovits (1966) found that people raised in carpentered environments full of right angles are more susceptible than groups such as the Zulu, who live in round huts and see fewer straight corners, the carpentered-world hypothesis. The standard criticism is that the illusion survives when the fins are swapped for circles or dots, which carry no depth cue, so Gregory is not the whole story. For the exam, the keyed answer is misapplied size constancy, also worded as depth cues or constancy scaling.", "traps": {"c": "Lateral inhibition (H. Keffer Hartline's work on the Limulus eye) explains brightness illusions at edges, such as Mach bands and the grey dots of the Hermann grid. It says nothing about perceived length.", "b": "Binocular disparity is a two-eye depth cue for stereopsis. The Müller-Lyer illusion works with one eye shut and on a flat page, so disparity cannot be its cause.", "d": "Figure-ground reversal is the Rubin vase (Edgar Rubin, 1915), a Gestalt organisation effect. Nothing in the Müller-Lyer figure flips between figure and ground."}}, {"q": "A lesion of the ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) produces:", "concept": "The answer is hyperphagia and obesity, the VMH syndrome first shown in rats by Albert Hetherington and Stephen Ranson (1940). The classic dual-centre model (Eliot Stellar, 1954) called the ventromedial hypothalamus the satiety centre and the lateral hypothalamus (LH) the feeding centre. Destroy the VMH and the animal overeats and becomes obese. Destroy the LH (Bal Krishan Anand and John Brobeck, 1951) and it stops eating and drinking. The VMH rat goes through two phases: a dynamic phase of heavy overeating and rapid weight gain, then a static phase where weight levels off at a new, high point and intake drops back toward normal. It also becomes a finicky eater, eating more of tasty food and less of bitter food than a normal rat. The modern view in John Pinel's Biopsychology is that the dual-centre idea is too simple. Much of the effect comes from damage to the nearby paraventricular nucleus and the fibre tracts passing the VMH, and VMH lesions raise blood insulin. High insulin drives lipogenesis (storing fat) and blocks lipolysis (releasing it), so the animal has to keep eating to have enough usable energy in the blood. Lock it as: VMH lesion makes the animal fat, LH lesion makes it thin.", "traps": {"b": "Aphagia (no eating) and adipsia (no drinking) follow a LATERAL hypothalamus lesion (Anand and Brobeck, 1951). This is the mirror-image trap: same region, opposite nucleus, opposite effect.", "a": "Circadian rhythms are driven by the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN), just above the optic chiasm. An SCN lesion (Friedrich Stephan and Irving Zucker, 1972) flattens the sleep-wake and drinking rhythms. That is a different nucleus from the VMH.", "c": "Diabetes insipidus comes from damage to the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei or the posterior pituitary, which make and release vasopressin (ADH). Without ADH the kidneys cannot hold water and the person passes large amounts of dilute urine. It is a water problem, not an eating one."}}, {"q": "Bilateral removal of the amygdala in monkeys produces:", "concept": "The answer is tameness and loss of fear, the Klüver-Bucy syndrome. Heinrich Klüver and Paul Bucy (1937, 1939) removed both temporal lobes in rhesus monkeys, and later work tied the emotional part of the syndrome to the amygdala. The full picture: tameness and loss of fear (the monkeys approached snakes and people they used to flee), psychic blindness or visual agnosia (they could see objects but not recognise what they meant), hyperorality (putting everything in the mouth), hypersexuality, dietary changes (eating meat and inedible things), and hypermetamorphosis (compulsively attending to and touching everything in view). The trap your stem sets: fear, attack posture and piloerection (hair standing on end) are what STIMULATION of the amygdala produces, the defensive rage response. A lesion takes them away. So if the options are fear, attack posture, piloerection and a fourth like tameness or loss of all of these, the stem word lesion points to the loss. In humans, bilateral amygdala damage in patient S.M. (Urbach-Wiethe disease, Ralph Adolphs and colleagues, 1994) wiped out recognising fear in faces and the feeling of fear itself. Joseph LeDoux's fear-conditioning circuit runs through the amygdala, which is why losing it removes learned fear.", "traps": {"b": "This is the stimulation picture, not the lesion picture. Electrically stimulating the amygdala gives fear, hissing, attack posture and piloerection. Read the stem verb: lesion means loss.", "a": "Hyperphagia and obesity follow a ventromedial hypothalamus lesion, item 2 of your set. Klüver-Bucy monkeys change WHAT they eat (meat, inedible objects), not how much.", "c": "Dense anterograde amnesia for facts and events comes from bilateral medial temporal lobe removal that includes the hippocampus (H.M., William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957). Losing the amygdala alone hits emotion and fear learning, not declarative memory."}}, {"q": "Scores of 8 students: 2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9. Using σ² = Σ(X − M)² / N, the variance is:", "concept": "The answer is 4. Step 1, the mean: M = ΣX / N = 40 / 8 = 5. Step 2, deviations X − M: −3, −1, −1, −1, 0, 0, 2, 4. Step 3, square them: 9, 1, 1, 1, 0, 0, 4, 16. Their sum, the sum of squares (SS), is 32. Step 4, divide by N: 32 / 8 = 4. The standard deviation is its square root, σ = 2. Variance is the average squared distance from the mean, so it is in squared units; the SD brings it back to score units. Two versions to know. Population variance σ² = SS / N is what Henry Garrett and most Indian psychometrics texts use for describing a set of scores. The unbiased sample estimate s² = SS / (N − 1) would give 32 / 7 = 4.57 here, and you use it when the paper says sample or estimate the population. Shortcut for raw data: σ² = ΣX² / N − M². Here ΣX² = 232, 232 / 8 = 29, and 29 − 25 = 4, the same answer. Quick rules: variance is never negative, it is zero only when every score is equal, adding a constant to every score leaves it unchanged, and multiplying every score by c multiplies it by c².", "traps": {"b": "2 is the standard deviation, the square root of the variance. Stopping at the SD when the paper asks for variance (or the reverse) is the most common slip on this question.", "d": "5 is the mean. Variance measures spread around the mean, not the mean itself.", "c": "32 is the sum of squared deviations (SS) before dividing by N. You did the hard part and skipped the last step."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is a non-linear transformation of raw scores?", "concept": "The answer is stanine. A linear transformation changes every score by the same multiply-and-add rule (new = a + b × old). It keeps the shape of the distribution, and the distances between scores stay in proportion, so a skewed raw distribution stays skewed. z = (X − M) / SD, deviation scores and the linear T-score 50 + 10z are all linear. A non-linear transformation, also called an area or normalising transformation, goes through percentile ranks: each raw score becomes the proportion of people below it, and that proportion is mapped onto a normal curve. This forces the distribution into a normal shape, so the spacing between scores changes. The non-linear family: percentile ranks, normalised standard scores, McCall's T-scale (William McCall, 1922, built as a normalised scale), stanines (standard nine, mean 5, SD about 2, first used by the US Army Air Forces in World War II), stens (standard ten, mean 5.5, SD 2) and normal-curve equivalents. The T trap: 50 + 10z is linear, while a normalised or McCall's T is non-linear, which is why some keys list T-scores as non-linear. Percentile ranks stretch and squeeze the most: near the middle of a normal curve a small raw-score change moves the percentile a lot, while in the tails a big raw change barely moves it.", "traps": {"a": "z = (X − M) / SD subtracts one constant and divides by another, a straight-line rule, so it is linear. A skewed raw distribution gives skewed z-scores.", "d": "Subtracting the mean from every score slides the whole distribution along the axis. The shape and the spacing between scores stay identical, so it is linear.", "b": "50 + 10z is the linear T-score: multiply by 10, add 50, still a straight line. Only the normalised T (McCall's original scale) is non-linear, and this option is the plain linear one."}}, {"q": "The Spearman-Brown prophecy formula is used to:", "concept": "The answer is d. Charles Spearman and William Brown published it independently in 1910, in the same issue of the British Journal of Psychology. Formula: r_new = k × r / (1 + (k − 1) × r), where r is the current reliability and k is how many times longer or shorter the new test is. Its commonest use is split-half reliability. You split one test into two halves (usually odd versus even items) and correlate them. That correlation is the reliability of a HALF-length test, and a shorter test is less reliable, so you step it up to full length with k = 2: r_full = 2r / (1 + r). The rules it rests on: the two halves, or the items being added, must be parallel to the existing ones (same content, same difficulty, equal means and equal variances), they must measure the same thing, and errors must be random and uncorrelated between halves. How it is applied: lengthening with parallel items always raises reliability, with diminishing returns; shortening (k less than 1) lowers it; and you can solve for k to find how long a test must be to reach a target, k = r_target (1 − r) / (r (1 − r_target)). Where it fails: when the halves have unequal variances it overestimates, so use Rulon's or Flanagan's formula instead, and on a speed test the halves correlate near 1 for the wrong reason, so the stepped-up split-half is spuriously high. The drill section of this mock has the numbers.", "traps": {"b": "Correcting a correlation for unreliability is Spearman's correction for attenuation (1904): r_true = r_xy / √(r_xx × r_yy). Same Spearman, different formula, and papers pair them on purpose.", "a": "Agreement between raters on categories is Cohen's kappa (Jacob Cohen, 1960), which corrects percent agreement for chance. Spearman-Brown is about test length, though it can project the reliability of an average across several raters.", "c": "This is the one thing Spearman-Brown cannot rescue. On a speed test the single-sitting split-half is already spuriously high, and stepping it up makes it worse. Speed tests need test-retest, alternate forms or separately timed halves."}}, {"q": "Kuder-Richardson Formula 20 (KR-20) is the appropriate reliability estimate when:", "concept": "The answer is b. G. Frederic Kuder and Marion Richardson (1937) derived a set of internal-consistency formulas from a single administration, and the 20th and 21st in their paper became the famous ones. KR-20 = (k / (k − 1)) × (1 − Σpq / σ²), where k is the number of items, p is the proportion passing each item, q = 1 − p, pq is that item's variance, and σ² is the variance of total scores. The logic: if the items all measure the same thing, they covary, so the total-score variance is much bigger than the sum of the separate item variances. Σpq / σ² is then small and KR-20 is high. It equals the average of all possible split-half coefficients, so no arbitrary split is needed. Conditions: items scored 0 or 1, one trait being measured (homogeneous content), and a power test, not a speed test. KR-21 is the shortcut that replaces Σpq with M(k − M) / k, which assumes every item has the same difficulty; when difficulties differ, KR-21 comes out lower than KR-20. Cronbach's alpha (Lee Cronbach, 1951) is the general version for any item format, and with 0/1 items alpha equals KR-20 exactly. A high KR-20 means the items hang together. It does not prove a single factor and it says nothing about stability over time.", "traps": {"d": "Likert items have more than two score points, so their variance is not pq. They call for Cronbach's alpha, which generalises KR-20 to any item format.", "c": "On a speed test nearly everyone gets every attempted item right and every unattempted item wrong, so internal consistency comes out falsely close to 1. KR-20 is for power tests.", "a": "Giving the same test twice, two weeks apart, is test-retest reliability, the coefficient of stability. KR-20 needs one administration and measures internal consistency."}}, {"q": "Rulon's formula estimates split-half reliability from:", "concept": "The answer is a. Phillip Rulon (1939): r = 1 − σ²_d / σ²_x, where d is each person's difference score (odd-half score minus even-half score) and σ²_x is the variance of total scores. The logic: if both halves measure the same true score, any difference between them is error. So the variance of the differences estimates error variance, and 1 minus the error share of total variance is reliability, straight from the classical definition r = 1 − σ²_e / σ²_x. Why it matters: it gives the full-test reliability directly, so you do NOT step it up with Spearman-Brown afterwards, and it does not need the two halves to have equal variances, which Spearman-Brown does. Flanagan's formula (John Flanagan, 1937) is the equivalent version written with the half variances, r = 2 (1 − (σ²_a + σ²_b) / σ²_x), and Louis Guttman's split-half coefficient (1945) is the same quantity. When the halves happen to have equal variances, Rulon, Flanagan and Spearman-Brown all give the same answer. Common exam angles: which split-half formula uses the variance of difference scores (Rulon), and which split-half formulas need no correlation coefficient (Rulon and Flanagan).", "traps": {"c": "That is the standard split-half route: correlate the halves, then step up with Spearman-Brown. Rulon's point is that you skip both the correlation and the step-up.", "d": "The proportion passing each item (p) feeds KR-20 and KR-21 through pq, not Rulon's formula.", "b": "Correlating two administrations is test-retest reliability. Rulon works on one administration split into two halves."}}, {"q": "For a pure speed test, the LEAST appropriate way to estimate reliability is:", "concept": "The answer is odd-even split-half from one sitting. A pure speed test has easy items and a tight time limit, so the score is simply how far you got. Almost everyone gets every item they reach right and every item they do not reach wrong. Split that into odd and even items and each person's two half-scores are nearly identical by construction: if you reached item 60, you have about 30 odd and 30 even right. The correlation between halves comes out near 1.00, not because the test is consistent but because of how speed scores are built. The same inflation hits KR-20, KR-21 and Cronbach's alpha, the other single-sitting internal-consistency methods; this is Anne Anastasi's standard warning in Psychological Testing. What to use instead: test-retest (coefficient of stability), alternate forms (coefficient of equivalence), or, if you must split, give the two halves separately with their own time limits, then correlate them and step up with Spearman-Brown. Exam variants: spuriously high reliability on a speed test comes from split-half or internal consistency; the most suitable methods for a speed test are test-retest and alternate forms.", "traps": {"b": "Test-retest is fine for a speed test. Speed of working is fairly stable over time, and the two sittings are separate, so nothing forces the scores to match. It is one of the recommended methods.", "a": "Alternate forms, each given on its own with its own time limit, are one of the best choices for a speed test.", "c": "Separately timed halves are the fix for split-half on a speed test: each half has its own time limit, so how far you got in one half does not decide your score on the other."}}, {"q": "Circumstantiality is a disorder of:", "concept": "The answer is form of thought. In circumstantiality the patient takes a long, roundabout route through unnecessary detail and side-tracks, but eventually reaches the point and answers the question. Contrast tangentiality, where the patient drifts off and never reaches the goal. It is seen in epilepsy (classically temporal lobe), obsessional personalities, intellectual disability, early dementia and in anxious or pedantic people, and a mild form can be normal. The four classic headings for disorders of thought come from Frank Fish's Clinical Psychopathology and are used in Indian texts: stream (speed and flow, such as flight of ideas, pressure of thought, poverty of thought, thought block), form (how thoughts link, such as loosening of association, derailment, tangentiality, circumstantiality, neologism, perseveration, word salad), content (what is thought, such as delusions, overvalued ideas, preoccupations) and possession (ownership of thought, such as thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting; Fish also puts obsessions here). Most keys, and Kaplan and Sadock, put circumstantiality under form, as a formal thought disorder. Fish himself listed it under disorders of the tempo of thought, which is stream. So: if form is an option, pick form; if only stream fits, pick stream. It is never content or possession.", "traps": {"b": "Content is WHAT the person thinks: delusions, overvalued ideas, preoccupations. Circumstantiality is about HOW the thinking travels to its goal, not what it is about.", "a": "Possession disorders concern ownership of thought: thought insertion, withdrawal and broadcasting (Kurt Schneider's first-rank symptoms), plus obsessions in Fish's scheme. A circumstantial patient fully owns the thoughts.", "d": "Disorders of perception are hallucinations, illusions and pseudohallucinations. Circumstantiality is a finding about thought and speech, noted under thought process in the mental status examination."}}, {"q": "The Rights of Persons with Disabilities (RPwD) Act was passed in ____ and recognises ____ disabilities.", "concept": "The answer is 2016 and 21. The RPwD Act, 2016 (Act No. 49 of 2016) received Presidential assent on 27 December 2016 and came into force on 19 April 2017. It replaced the Persons with Disabilities (Equal Opportunities, Protection of Rights and Full Participation) Act, 1995, which recognised only 7 disabilities: blindness, low vision, leprosy-cured, hearing impairment, locomotor disability, mental retardation and mental illness. It was passed to bring Indian law in line with the UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (UNCRPD, 2006), which India ratified in 2007. The 21 are: blindness, low vision, leprosy-cured persons, hearing impairment (deaf and hard of hearing), locomotor disability, dwarfism, intellectual disability, mental illness, autism spectrum disorder, cerebral palsy, muscular dystrophy, chronic neurological conditions, specific learning disabilities, multiple sclerosis, speech and language disability, thalassemia, haemophilia, sickle cell disease, multiple disabilities including deaf-blindness, acid attack victims, and Parkinson's disease. Benchmark disability means at least 40% of a specified disability, which unlocks reservation: 4% of government posts (Section 34) and 5% of seats in government and aided higher education (Section 32). Points that matter for psychology: the Act uses intellectual disability in place of mental retardation, it added autism and specific learning disabilities, and the central government can notify further categories.", "traps": {"a": "1995 and 7 belong to the old Persons with Disabilities Act, 1995, which the RPwD Act replaced. Papers use it as the decoy pair.", "d": "2017 is when the Act came into force (19 April 2017), and also the year of the Mental Healthcare Act. The Act itself is of 2016.", "b": "19 is a decoy; the count is 21. The 2016 Act added 14 categories to the old 7."}}, {"q": "The right to make an advance directive about mental health treatment is given by the:", "concept": "The answer is the Mental Healthcare Act (MHCA), 2017, Act No. 10 of 2017, assented on 7 April 2017 and in force from 29 May 2018. Chapter III, Sections 5 to 13, lets every adult write an advance directive saying how they want, or do not want, to be cared for and treated for a mental illness, and Section 14 lets them appoint a nominated representative. The directive is made in writing, can be changed or revoked at any time, is registered with the Mental Health Review Board, and does not apply to emergency treatment. Other high-yield MHCA sections: Section 18, the right to access mental healthcare; Section 89, supported admission for up to 30 days; Section 94, emergency treatment for up to 72 hours; Section 95, banned procedures (ECT without anaesthesia and muscle relaxant, ECT for minors unless the guardian consents and the Board permits, sterilisation as treatment, chaining); and Section 115, which presumes severe stress in anyone who attempts suicide, so the attempt is not punished. The Act replaced the Mental Health Act, 1987 and set up the Central and State Mental Health Authorities and the Review Boards. Both the MHCA and the RPwD Act grew out of the UNCRPD, which is why papers pair them.", "traps": {"c": "The RPwD Act, 2016 covers disability rights, reservation and the 21 disabilities (items 11 and 13 of your set). It has no advance directive provision.", "b": "The Mental Health Act, 1987 was the older law on admission to and licensing of psychiatric hospitals. It had no advance directives, and the MHCA 2017 replaced it.", "d": "The RCI Act, 1992 created the Rehabilitation Council of India, which regulates the training and registration of rehabilitation professionals, clinical psychologists included. It says nothing about patients' treatment choices."}}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT listed as a specified disability under the RPwD Act, 2016?", "concept": "The answer is ADHD. The Act's schedule groups its 21 specified disabilities under five heads. Physical disability covers locomotor disability (including leprosy-cured persons, cerebral palsy, dwarfism, muscular dystrophy and acid attack victims), visual impairment (blindness, low vision), hearing impairment (deaf, hard of hearing) and speech and language disability. Intellectual disability has specific learning disabilities and autism spectrum disorder listed under it. Mental behaviour covers mental illness. Disability caused by chronic neurological conditions (multiple sclerosis, Parkinson's disease) and blood disorders (haemophilia, thalassemia, sickle cell disease) comes next, and the last head is multiple disabilities, including deaf-blindness. ADHD is not named anywhere in the schedule. The trick in this question is that all four options are neurodevelopmental disorders in DSM-5, and three of them made it into the Act. The specific learning disabilities named in the Act are dyslexia, dysgraphia, dyscalculia, dyspraxia, developmental aphasia and perceptual disabilities.", "traps": {"d": "Autism spectrum disorder is listed, under the intellectual disability head of the schedule. It is one of the categories the 2016 Act added over the 1995 Act.", "b": "Intellectual disability is listed. It replaced the 1995 Act's term mental retardation.", "a": "Specific learning disabilities (dyslexia, dysgraphia, dyscalculia, dyspraxia, developmental aphasia) are listed, also under the intellectual disability head."}}, {"q": "In James Gross's process model of emotion regulation, cognitive reappraisal belongs to which family of strategies?", "concept": "The answer is cognitive change. Your stem was cut off at Emotional Regulatio, so this is the emotion-regulation question papers ask most. James Gross (1998) defines emotion regulation as the processes by which we influence which emotions we have, when we have them, and how we experience and express them. His process model has five families of strategies, ordered by when they act along the emotion's timeline. Situation selection: choosing to enter or avoid a situation, like skipping a party you dread. Situation modification: changing the situation itself, like asking a friend to come along. Attentional deployment: shifting attention, as in distraction or rumination. Cognitive change: changing how you appraise the situation, and cognitive reappraisal is the main example (the interviewer is tired, not hostile). Response modulation: acting on the emotion once it is under way, as in expressive suppression, exercise or drugs. The first four are antecedent-focused, acting before the emotion fully forms; the fifth is response-focused. Gross and Oliver John (2003) built the 10-item Emotion Regulation Questionnaire (ERQ) to measure reappraisal and suppression. Reappraisal lowers the felt negative emotion and goes with better wellbeing. Suppression hides the expression but not the feeling, raises physiological arousal and hurts memory and relationships. Clinical link: Marsha Linehan's dialectical behaviour therapy has an emotion regulation skills module, and emotion dysregulation is the core problem in her biosocial model of borderline personality disorder.", "traps": {"c": "Situation selection acts earliest of all, before you are in the situation (deciding whether to go). Reappraisal happens once you are in it, by changing what it means to you.", "d": "Attentional deployment changes WHERE your attention goes (distraction, concentration, rumination), not what the situation means. Its classic example is distraction.", "a": "Response modulation is the late, response-focused family, and its textbook example is expressive suppression, the strategy Gross always contrasts WITH reappraisal. This is the main trap."}}, {"q": "The correlation between the odd and even halves of a test is .60. By the Spearman-Brown formula, the reliability of the full test is:", "concept": "The answer is .75. The split-half correlation of .60 is the reliability of a half-length test. To get the full test you double the length, so k = 2: r_full = 2r / (1 + r) = 2 × .60 / (1 + .60) = 1.20 / 1.60 = .75. The general formula is r_new = k r / (1 + (k − 1) r). Sanity check: the stepped-up value is always higher than the half-test r, because lengthening helps, but it never reaches 1.", "traps": {"b": ".60 is the half-test reliability, the raw split-half correlation. Reporting it as the full-test value underestimates reliability, the exact mistake Spearman-Brown exists to fix.", "a": ".80 comes from guessing or misremembering the formula. Work it through: 1.20 / 1.60 = .75.", "d": ".36 is .60 squared, the variance the two halves share (r²). Squaring belongs to the coefficient of determination, not to reliability."}}, {"q": "A test has a reliability of .50. If it is made three times as long with parallel items, its predicted reliability is:", "concept": "The answer is .75. Here k = 3, so r_new = k r / (1 + (k − 1) r) = 3 × .50 / (1 + 2 × .50) = 1.50 / 2.00 = .75. Notice the diminishing returns: doubling (k = 2) gives 1.00 / 1.50 = .67, tripling gives .75, and five times the length gives 2.50 / 3.00 = .83. Each extra block of items adds less, and reliability only approaches 1 as the length heads to infinity.", "traps": {"b": ".60 underestimates the gain. Put k = 3 into the formula properly: 1.50 / 2.00.", "a": ".83 is the answer for k = 5 (2.50 / 3.00). Right formula, wrong k.", "c": "1.00 comes from multiplying 3 × .50 = 1.50 and capping it. The denominator 1 + (k − 1) r is there so a finite test can never reach 1."}}, {"q": "A test has a reliability of .60. How many times longer must it be made, with parallel items, to reach a reliability of .90?", "concept": "The answer is 6 times. Rearranged Spearman-Brown: k = r_target (1 − r_old) / (r_old (1 − r_target)) = .90 × .40 / (.60 × .10) = .36 / .06 = 6. Check by plugging it back in: 6 × .60 / (1 + 5 × .60) = 3.60 / 4.00 = .90. So a 20-item test would need 120 parallel items. This is why a short screening tool with reliability around .60 cannot be pushed to the .90 level needed for individual clinical decisions just by adding a few items.", "traps": {"c": "1.5 is .90 / .60, a plain ratio of the two reliabilities. Reliability does not grow in proportion to length.", "a": "3 falls short. Plug it back in: 3 × .60 / (1 + 2 × .60) = 1.80 / 2.20 = .82, not .90.", "d": "9 is .90 / .10, using only the target's numbers. The formula needs both the old and the target reliability."}}, {"q": "A test with a reliability of .80 is cut to half its length. The predicted reliability of the short form is:", "concept": "The answer is .67. Spearman-Brown works for shortening too, with k less than 1. With k = 0.5: r_new = 0.5 × .80 / (1 + (0.5 − 1) × .80) = .40 / (1 − .40) = .40 / .60 = .67. It is the reverse of the split-half step-up, and you can check it: stepping .67 back up with k = 2 gives 1.33 / 1.67 = .80. Use it whenever someone wants a short form of a test.", "traps": {"a": ".40 halves the reliability itself. Reliability does not fall in proportion to length; the denominator softens the drop.", "d": ".80 assumes length does not matter. It does: with fewer items, error makes up a bigger share of each score.", "c": ".89 is the DOUBLING answer (1.60 / 1.80). The test is being halved, so k = 0.5, not 2."}}, {"q": "The Spearman-Brown correction of a split-half coefficient is accurate only when the two halves:", "concept": "The answer is a. Spearman-Brown assumes the halves are parallel tests: the same true score and equal error variance, which means equal means and equal observed variances. Only then is the correlation between halves a fair estimate of each half's reliability. If the halves have unequal variances, for example one half holding most of the hard items, Spearman-Brown overestimates reliability. Then you use Rulon's formula (1939), 1 − σ²_d / σ²_x, or Flanagan's (1937), 2 (1 − (σ²_a + σ²_b) / σ²_x), which only assume the halves measure the same true score, not that their variances match. How to make halves parallel: an odd-even split spreads difficulty evenly when items run from easy to hard, or match items on content and difficulty. A first-half versus second-half split is the worst, because fatigue, practice and time pressure all fall on the second half.", "traps": {"b": "Halves that differ in difficulty are exactly the case where Spearman-Brown goes wrong: unequal halves give unequal variances and an overestimate.", "d": "Splitting across two days turns it into a test-retest design and mixes in day-to-day change. Split-half is a single-sitting method.", "c": "Different scorers bring in rater error, which is an inter-scorer reliability question. It is not what the formula assumes about the halves."}}, {"q": "On a test split into odd and even halves, the variance of the difference scores (odd minus even) is 4 and the variance of total scores is 20. By Rulon's formula, the reliability is:", "concept": "The answer is .80. Rulon: r = 1 − σ²_d / σ²_x = 1 − 4 / 20 = 1 − .20 = .80. The difference between the halves is treated as pure error, so 4 / 20 = .20 is the error share of the total variance, and the rest is reliable variance. Rulon's result is already the full-test reliability, so there is no Spearman-Brown step afterwards.", "traps": {"a": ".20 is the error share, σ²_d / σ²_x. You stopped one step early: reliability is 1 minus that.", "d": ".89 is .80 stepped up again with Spearman-Brown (1.60 / 1.80). Rulon already gives the full-test reliability, so stepping up counts the length twice.", "c": ".96 comes from squaring the ratio (1 − .20²). The formula uses the variance ratio as it is."}}, {"q": "In a split-half analysis, the standard deviation of the difference scores is 3 and the standard deviation of total scores is 6. Rulon's reliability coefficient is:", "concept": "The answer is .75. Rulon's formula needs VARIANCES, so square the SDs first: σ²_d = 3² = 9 and σ²_x = 6² = 36. Then r = 1 − 9 / 36 = 1 − .25 = .75. Papers give SDs on purpose to catch anyone who plugs them straight in.", "traps": {"c": ".50 is 1 − 3 / 6, using the SDs directly. The formula is a ratio of variances, so square first.", "d": ".25 is the error share, 9 / 36. Reliability is 1 minus it.", "b": ".86 is .75 stepped up with Spearman-Brown (1.50 / 1.75). Rulon already gives the full-length value, so there is no step-up."}}, {"q": "A 10-item right/wrong test has Σpq = 1.8 and a total-score variance of 6. Its KR-20 reliability is about:", "concept": "The answer is .78. KR-20 = (k / (k − 1)) × (1 − Σpq / σ²). Step 1: Σpq / σ² = 1.8 / 6 = .30. Step 2: 1 − .30 = .70. Step 3: the correction factor k / (k − 1) = 10 / 9 = 1.111. Step 4: 1.111 × .70 = .78. The k / (k − 1) factor matters most on short tests: with 10 items it adds about 11%, with 100 items only about 1%.", "traps": {"c": ".70 leaves out the k / (k − 1) correction, an easy slip because 1 − .30 looks like the end of the sum.", "a": ".30 is Σpq / σ², the item-variance share. You stopped before taking 1 minus it.", "d": ".63 multiplies by 9 / 10 instead of 10 / 9. The factor is k over k minus 1, which is always greater than 1."}}, {"q": "On a right/wrong item, 30% of examinees answer correctly. The item variance (pq) that goes into KR-20 is:", "concept": "The answer is .21. For a dichotomous item, p is the proportion correct (.30), q = 1 − p = .70, and the item variance is p × q = .30 × .70 = .21. Item variance peaks at p = .50 (.50 × .50 = .25) and falls to 0 when everyone passes or everyone fails. That is why items of middling difficulty, p around .50, carry the most information and discriminate best, and why item analysis prefers them. In KR-20 you add pq across all the items to get Σpq.", "traps": {"b": ".09 is p² (.30 × .30). Item variance is p times q, not p times p.", "d": ".30 is just p, the difficulty index. The variance needs q as well.", "a": ".25 is the MAXIMUM item variance, reached only at p = .50. This item is harder than that, so its variance is lower."}}, {"q": "A 20-item test has a mean of 12 and a variance of 16. Its KR-21 reliability is about:", "concept": "The answer is .74. KR-21 = (k / (k − 1)) × (1 − M (k − M) / (k σ²)), and it needs only the number of items, the mean and the variance, with no item-by-item data. Step 1: M (k − M) = 12 × 8 = 96. Step 2: k σ² = 20 × 16 = 320. Step 3: 96 / 320 = .30, so 1 − .30 = .70. Step 4: multiply by 20 / 19 = 1.053, which gives .74. KR-21 assumes every item has the same difficulty. Real items never do, so KR-21 comes out lower than KR-20 and works as a quick, conservative estimate.", "traps": {"b": ".70 leaves out the k / (k − 1) = 20 / 19 correction.", "a": ".30 is the ratio M (k − M) / (k σ²). You stopped before taking 1 minus it.", "c": ".85 is .74 stepped up with Spearman-Brown. The Kuder-Richardson formulas already give full-test reliability and are never stepped up."}}, {"q": "When the items of a test differ in difficulty, KR-21 compared with KR-20 gives:", "concept": "The answer is a lower estimate. KR-21 swaps the real Σpq for k × p̄q̄, the value Σpq would take if every item had the average difficulty. Because pq is largest at p = .50 and curves down on both sides, spreading item difficulties around the average always makes the real Σpq SMALLER than k × p̄q̄. KR-20 subtracts the smaller real value, so it comes out higher; KR-21 subtracts the larger assumed value, so it comes out lower. The two are equal only when every item has exactly the same difficulty. So KR-21 is a lower-bound shortcut: if KR-21 already looks good, KR-20 will be at least as good.", "traps": {"d": "Backwards. Assuming equal difficulty overstates the summed item variance, which pulls KR-21 down, not up.", "a": "They match only in the special case where every item has identical difficulty, and the stem says the difficulties differ.", "b": "Both Kuder-Richardson formulas measure internal consistency from one sitting. Neither measures stability over time; that is test-retest."}}, {"q": "Cronbach's coefficient alpha is identical to KR-20 when:", "concept": "The answer is b. Lee Cronbach (1951) generalised KR-20 to any item format: α = (k / (k − 1)) × (1 − Σσ²_i / σ²_x), where σ²_i is each item's variance. For a 0/1 item the item variance is exactly pq, so Σσ²_i = Σpq and alpha becomes KR-20 term for term. Cronbach also showed that alpha is the mean of all possible split-half coefficients (computed the Rulon-Flanagan way), and it is a lower bound to true reliability when items are not equally good measures of the trait. Use alpha for Likert and rating items and KR-20 for right/wrong items. Jum Nunnally's rule of thumb: .70 is acceptable for early research, .80 for most research, and .90 or above for decisions about individuals.", "traps": {"a": "On a speed test alpha and KR-20 are both spuriously high, but that does not make them equal. The equality comes from the item format.", "d": "Equal half variances is the condition for Spearman-Brown to agree with Rulon and Flanagan. That is the split-half family, not alpha versus KR-20.", "c": "Giving the test twice is test-retest reliability. Alpha and KR-20 both come from a single administration."}}, {"q": "A clerical speed test has to be split in half to estimate its reliability. The acceptable procedure is to:", "concept": "The answer is a. On a speed test, any split inside one timed sitting gives two nearly identical half-scores, because everyone gets right whatever they reach and the score only tracks how far they got. The fix, Anne Anastasi's standard recommendation, is to give each half as its own timed test with half the time limit. Then how far you get in half A does not decide your score on half B, so the correlation reflects real consistency. Correct that half-test r to full length with Spearman-Brown (k = 2). The other good options for speed tests avoid splitting altogether: test-retest and alternate forms.", "traps": {"b": "This is the classic inflated estimate. In one timed sitting the odd and even scores match almost perfectly, so r comes out near 1.00 for reasons that have nothing to do with reliability.", "d": "KR-20 is single-sitting internal consistency and is just as inflated on a speed test as an odd-even split.", "c": "Alpha is the general form of KR-20 and has the same problem on speeded tests: it reads speed as consistency."}}, {"q": "Scores of 3, 5 and 7 are a sample. The unbiased estimate of the population variance (dividing by N − 1) is:", "concept": "The answer is 4. Mean = 15 / 3 = 5. Deviations: −2, 0, 2. Squares: 4, 0, 4, so SS = 8. The unbiased sample variance is s² = SS / (N − 1) = 8 / 2 = 4. Why N − 1: deviations from a sample's own mean are always a little smaller than deviations from the true population mean, because the sample mean sits in the middle of its own data, so dividing by N underestimates the population variance. Dividing by N − 1 (Bessel's correction) removes that bias. N − 1 is also the degrees of freedom: once you know the mean and two of the three deviations, the third is fixed.", "traps": {"a": "2.67 divides by N (8 / 3). That describes these three scores but is not the unbiased population estimate the stem asks for.", "c": "2 is the SD, the square root of 4. The question asks for the variance.", "d": "8 is SS before dividing."}}, {"q": "A set of scores has a variance of 4. Every score is multiplied by 3 and then 10 is added. The new variance is:", "concept": "The answer is 36. Adding a constant shifts every score and the mean by the same amount, so every deviation from the mean stays the same and the variance does not change. Multiplying by a constant c multiplies every deviation by c, so the squared deviations, and the variance, are multiplied by c². Here 4 × 3² = 4 × 9 = 36, and the +10 changes nothing. The SD goes from 2 to 6, multiplied by 3. The mean takes both changes: new mean = 3 × old mean + 10. This is the rule behind linear transformations (item 5 of your recall set): z, linear T and deviation-score scales change the mean and SD this way without changing the shape of the distribution.", "traps": {"b": "4 assumes the transformation leaves variance alone. Adding a constant does; multiplying does not.", "c": "12 multiplies the variance by 3. Variance is in squared units, so it scales by 3² = 9. It is the SD that scales by 3.", "d": "46 adds the 10 to the variance (36 + 10). Adding a constant moves the mean, not the spread."}}, {"q": "A test has an SD of 15 and a reliability of .91. The standard error of measurement (SEM) is:", "concept": "The answer is 4.5. SEM = SD × √(1 − r) = 15 × √(1 − .91) = 15 × √.09 = 15 × .30 = 4.5. The SEM is the SD of the errors you would get if one person took the test over and over, so it gives a band around the observed score where the true score probably lies: about 68% of the time within ±1 SEM (±4.5) and 95% within ±1.96 SEM (about ±8.8). On this test an observed IQ of 110 means the true IQ is probably between about 101 and 119. Higher reliability means a smaller SEM: at r = 1 the SEM is 0, and at r = 0 it equals the SD.", "traps": {"b": "1.35 is 15 × .09, leaving out the square root of (1 − r).", "c": "13.65 is 15 × .91, multiplying the SD by the reliability itself. The SEM uses 1 − r, under a square root.", "a": "0.3 is √(1 − r) on its own. You have to multiply by the SD to put it back in score units."}}, {"q": "A predictor and a criterion correlate .40. Their reliabilities are .64 and .81. Corrected for attenuation, the correlation between their true scores is about:", "concept": "The answer is .56. Charles Spearman's correction for attenuation (1904): r_true = r_xy / √(r_xx × r_yy) = .40 / √(.64 × .81) = .40 / (.80 × .90) = .40 / .72 = .56. Measurement error in either test drags the observed correlation down, attenuating it, and the formula estimates what the correlation would be with perfectly reliable measures. Two follow-ups papers like: a test's validity coefficient can never exceed the square root of its reliability (here √.64 = .80 is the ceiling for the predictor), and in validity studies you often correct only for the criterion's unreliability (.40 / √.81 = .40 / .90 = .44), so the test is not credited with a perfection it does not have.", "traps": {"d": ".40 is the observed correlation before any correction. The stem asks for the true-score correlation.", "a": ".77 divides by .64 × .81 = .52 without taking the square root.", "b": ".29 multiplies by .72 instead of dividing, so it pushes the correlation down instead of correcting it up."}}, {"q": "On the stanine scale, what percentage of a normal distribution falls in stanine 5?", "concept": "The answer is 20%. The stanine (standard nine) scale cuts the normal curve into nine bands, each half an SD wide except the two open-ended ends, with a mean of 5 and an SD of about 2. The percentages from stanine 1 to 9 are 4, 7, 12, 17, 20, 17, 12, 7, 4, adding to 100. Stanine 5 is the middle band, from −0.25 to +0.25 SD, and holds 20%. Stanines are assigned from percentile ranks, so they are a normalising, non-linear transformation (item 5 of your recall set). They trade precision for robustness: one digit, no false precision. The scale was developed for the US Army Air Forces in World War II. Its cousin, the sten (standard ten, mean 5.5, SD 2), is used by Raymond Cattell's 16PF.", "traps": {"b": "10% would be the share if the nine bands were equal. They are cut by SD on a normal curve, so the middle band is the widest in people.", "a": "17% is stanines 4 and 6, the bands either side of the middle.", "c": "25% is a quartile, a different scale. No stanine band holds a quarter of the curve."}}]});
